FIGURE 8.  Conceptual framework of CDKN2A expression during persistent LPS senescence induction. Persistent cholangiocyte stress results in the activation of NRAS and subsequently the MAPK/ERK signaling pathway. ETS1 is upregulated, phosphorylated and translocates to the nucleus.  In the nucleus, epigenetic histone modification (e.g., H3K4me3) allows p-ETS1 to bind to the CDKN2A promoter resulting in CDKN2A transcription and, ultimately, cellular senescence.  Pharmacologic inhibition of RAS/MEK/ERK (Manumycin A, PD98059 or UO126) and suppression of ETS1 (Ets1-shRNA and genome editing) prevents CDKN2A transcription and senescence.
